  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/a4/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_116.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "We're reintroduced to Nightmare, king of the dream dimension, who wants to kidnap humans while they sleep so he can study and learn how to defeat them, so he can \"conquer <i>their</i> dimension as well as my own.\"&nbsp; But Doctor Strange and the Ancient One stand in his way.\n\nMeanwhile, in Greenwich Village, a policeman and a Dr. Warren arrive to talk to Strange about the mysterious rash of people who have fallen asleep and can't be awakened.&nbsp; Though the two struggle with the notion that mystic arts are involved, they acknowledge that Strange has had \"an amazing record of success with <i>other</i> offbeat cases\" and defer to his fame and expertise.&nbsp; Doctor Strange consents to this and directs them to take him to the latest victim for a closer look.\n\nSoon, at the bedside of the slumbering victim, Strange uses his enchanted amulet and detects a mystic glow around him.&nbsp; He's in a \"magically-induced spell!\"&nbsp; It's a clear case of supernatural forces at play here, and Strange decides to return to his study to ponder his next steps.\n\nBack in his sanctum, he states he possesses the only known copy of the \"Book of Vishanti,\" which contains every counter-spell known to the mystic arts.&nbsp; He finds what he's looking for, but the words are very faint.&nbsp; One mistake in reading them aloud could lead to disaster.&nbsp; He'll have to read it slowly and deliberately.&nbsp; It contains mentions of Dormammu, the All-Seeing Agamotto and the Hosts of Hoggoth.&nbsp; This opens a portal to the \"Nightmare World.\"\n\nAs Strange proceeds along the path provided by the spell of Hoggoth, a door opens.&nbsp; He knows it's a trap, but he enters and confronts Nightmare!&nbsp; \"So, Doctor Strange, we meet again!\"&nbsp; Nightmare has three hostages -- the humans he has trapped in the dreamlike state -- and he attempts to disrupt the Path of Hoggoth so Strange can't reach them.&nbsp; But though Strange manages it, he steps off the path for just a moment, leaving Nightmare free to trap him, ultimately threatening him with a \"Spinybeast, whose touch means death!\"\n\nBut Strange shines the Amulet on Nightmare and his attacking beast, long enough to allow his return to the Path of Hoggoth, where he escapes the realm with the hostages.&nbsp; All have been saved, leaving Nightmare to cry \"We shall meet again... and next time, the victory shall be <i>mine</i>!!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "A tale that waves its hands a lot but never decides what it's actually doing.\nThe story collapses under the weight of its own improvisation.",
  "subtitle": "The Nightmare World's Unfinished Basement",
  "review_text": "Nightmare originally appeared in the very first Doctor Strange story back in [LINK:1963:ST_110_strange2|ST #110strange|2026-09-20], published six months earlier, and now that Marvel knows readers will actually <i>buy</i> a Doctor Strange-oriented feature (as stated in the splash page text insert), it made sense to bring him back, especially as that first story was very brief at only five pages long.\n\nBut it was important to remind readers that the villain is opposed by two main \"heroes,\" who are Doctor Strange and the Ancient One (yet the latter never appears here):\n[ASSET: center | NightmareIsBack | width=67%]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | WontWakeUp | width=12%] After being approached to look into a case involving a man who cannot be awakened, Strange detects evidence of supernatural forces.\n[BR]\n[BR] It's interesting that, when confronted by a man who won't wake up, Doctor Strange heads straight for his Book of Vishanti, convinced that his only means of stopping the supernatural interference is by reading aloud one of its most difficult incantations, and we're told that if he does it wrong, \"it will mean my doom!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=It's a startling escalation of events over the course of nine panels that ultimately leads him into the \"Nightmare World.\"]\n\n[ASSET: left | EnteringTheNightmareWorld | width=11%] [ASSET: right | SoWeMeetAgain | width=11%] When Nightmare confronts Strange face-to-face on page 5, he says, \"So, Doctor Strange, we meet again!\"&nbsp; This sounds very much like he's encountering him for only the second time, yet back in [LINK:1963:ST_110_strange2|ST #110strange|2026-09-20], Strange referred to him as \"my ancient foe.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] One would think that this latest meeting would be merely one of <i>many</i> such encounters, but the wording makes it play differently.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nFrom the moment Strange enters the Nightmare World, the rules of engagement with the enemy seem made up on the spot, and the reader is simply presented with them [SPAN: severity=info; text=as if they'd been carved into mystic stone tablets somewhere.]\n\nFor example:\n[HANG] * Strange must remain on the narrow path of Hoggoth, lest he be subjected to danger.\n[BR]\n[BR]\n[HANG] * Nightmare has three hostages who are \"free to go,\" but if Strange can't reach them, \"<i>all</i> your lives are forfeit!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] Remember that on page 3, Strange was approached by police because of the increasing \"number of people who cannot be awakened after they have fallen asleep.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=That number is <i>three</i>?]&nbsp; And somehow, <i>that</i> was enough to make the front page of the <i>Daily Globe</i> (as seen on the last panel of page 2)?\n[BR]\n[BR] While it's true that finding people who won't wake up is concerning, running to enlist the help of an occult specialist like Doctor Strange after only three occurrences seems like [SPAN: severity=warning; text=jumping the gun a bit.]\n[BR]\n[BR]\n[HANG] * Nightmare can't affect the path <i>behind</i> Doctor Strange, but he can destroy the path <i>ahead</i> of him.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I assume this is all documented in some \"<i>How to Engage Your Ancient Enemy in the Nightmare World for DUMMIES</i>\" handbook?)]\n[BR]\n[BR]\n[HANG] * Strange is safe to walk on the sash he unfurls on the broken path ahead of him, but though Nightmare can destroy the <i>actual </i>path of Hoggoth, he somehow can't affect the sash.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Why Not?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(You're telling me a <i>sash</i> is more invincible than the mystic Path of Hoggoth itself?&nbsp; <b><i>What?</i></b>)]\n[BR]\n[BR]\n[HANG] * Once Strange steps off the sash, he is captured by Nightmare, who summons the \"Spinybeast\" to touch him, never once attacking Strange himself, even though page 7 shows he can erect a mystic barrier.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Why not attack Strange <i>directly</i>?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And nice <i>beast</i>, by the way -- where can <i>I</i> get one?&nbsp; Oh, I <i>forgot</i>:&nbsp; Ditko is making this up as he goes!)]\n[BR]\n[BR]\n[HANG] * Strange retreats from the \"Spinybeast\" but has to stop, lest he fall down a crevice that is all-too conveniently located.\n[BR]\n[BR]\n[HANG] * Out of the blue, Strange directs the beam of his trusty amulet to kill the beast and also temporarily blinds Nightmare, allowing Strange to get back to the path of Hoggoth and escape to the mortal realm.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=What happened to the barrier that blocked him from it one page earlier?]\n\nAll of this comes across as [SPAN: severity=error; text=<b>extremely</b> arbitrary], and what seems more likely is that Stan and Steve are simply making this up as they go along.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhat is supposed to come across as being very mystical is actually a nonsensical sequence of events where the rules are basically whatever artist Steve Ditko can conjure up on the spot, leaving Stan free to toss in a few magical-sounding words to give it all the air of something far more significant than it really is...\n\n...which is nothing more than the villain shouting out the same tired refrain, essentially:\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=\"I'll get you <i>next</i> time, Doctor Strange!\"]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Spare me...)]\n\n[ASSET: center | IllGetYouNextTime | width=67%]\n\nPlus, since the page count had run its course, we're left to assume that Strange got back to the Dr. Warren he left behind on page 3 and that all three victims woke up, freed at last from their... <i>uh...</i> <b>nightmare</b>!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nAs I mentioned before, I had never read any of the Silver Age Doctor Strange adventures, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=and this one did nothing to make me question that decision.]\n\nThe story (such as it was) had very little going for it and, as I mentioned above, almost seemed made up panel by panel, with no sense of where any of it was supposed to lead, all while trying to visually be very \"mystical\" [SPAN: severity=success; text=(and in that respect, it <b>was</b>, so I give Stan and Steve credit for at least <i>that</i> much)].\n\nBut a story still has to have [SPAN: severity=info; text=some measure of internal logic], and conjuring up rules on the spot while allowing Strange to walk away with the only three humans Nightmare trapped in this dream state when page 2 made it crystal clear that the villain's ultimate goal was to study humans \"and learn how to <i>defeat</i> them\" so that he could \"conquer <i>their</i> dimension\" as well as his own!\n\nBut all that evil intention morphed <i>entirely </i>over to:\n[HANG] \"I must defeat Doctor Strange!\" and\n[HANG] \"Curse you, Doctor Strange!&nbsp; I'll get you <i>next</i> time!\"\n\nPut aside all the mystical accoutrements and incantation-laden speech (all present solely to provide an <i>atmosphere</i> of mysticism), and [SPAN: severity=info; text=you're left with nothing more than a stock villain who could have appeared in <i>any</i> magazine.]&nbsp; I never once got the impression that the co-creators had any earthly idea of where they were going with this.\n\nIn other words, [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=there was no meat on that bone!]\n[BR] And I yawned my way through it.\n\nI sincerely hope this is not what Doctor Strange stories are destined to be like, because if so, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=this will be tedious...]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n<b>Side Observation:&nbsp; </b>\n[BR] I know some readers are more taken with Doctor Strange's mystic atmosphere than I am, and that's fine -- there's certainly an appeal to the candles, the incantations, and the dream‑realms. But my comments here are aimed at how [SPAN: severity=info; text=this particular story] was put together.\n\nWhen the plot doesn't give the mysticism anything solid to stand on, the visuals can only carry it so far.&nbsp; If future installments tighten the structure, [SPAN: severity=success; text=I'll be the first to say so.]",
  "side_note": [
    "Pages 1 and 2 hint that Doctor Strange is world-renowned and has a reputation for being able to help with \"offbeat cases.\"&nbsp; I guess his \"shadowy, candlelit apartment in a back street of Greenwich Village\" (as quoted from page 2) only serves as a visual aid to his qualifications in that regard, <i>heh</i>."
  ],
  "highlight": "Doctor Strange stumbles through Nightmare's realm, where every mystic \"rule\" feels like it was invented five seconds before he arrived."
}